<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">'s-Hertogenbosch</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Den Bosch</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">'s-Hertogenbosch, also called Den Bosch, is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-brabant">North Brabant</a>. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-i-duke-of-brabant">Henry I of Brabant</a> granted it town rights around 1185, and it became one of the four chief towns of the duchy, a prosperous trading town and the home of the painter Hieronymus Bosch. Taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> after a siege in 1629, it is known for its Gothic St John's Cathedral.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/s-hertogenbosch.jpg" alt="'s-Hertogenbosch" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ingo Ronner · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Jans_cathedral_%27s-Hertogenbosch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
